Subject: Regexp matchers generated ahead of time from a pattern's bytecode are found by the exact bytecode and run in place of the interpreter

What this does
  - the interpreter's execution context, character access macros and helpers move from `libregexp.c` to a new header `libregexp-exec.h` (no change of text), so a matcher generated by `qjsaot` shares them
  - `libregexp.h`: `LreAotEntry` {bytecode, length, 8-bit matcher, 16-bit matcher}, `lre_aot_register` (a unit's constructor), `lre_aot_find` (open-addressing table on an FNV hash of the bytecode, compared byte for byte), `lre_exec_ex` and `lre_exec_at_ex` (the same entry points with an optional matcher)
  - `lre_exec_internal` runs the matcher in place of `lre_exec_backtrack` when it is given one for the subject width; a matcher that has no code for the entry offset returns `LRE_RET_AOT_NOENTRY` and the interpreter runs
  - `quickjs.c`: `JSReReq` (the per-context analysis kept by bytecode string) carries the matcher found once for the bytecode; the generic, bounded, prefix and first-set strategies pass it down
  - the regexp census (`QJS_RE_STATS`) is also switched on by `QJS_AOT_PROFILE_OUT`; the profile gets one `X <matcher cycles> <attempts on 16-bit subjects> <hex bytecode>` line per pattern that ran, which is what the generator selects matchers from

Why we need it
  The regexp row spends 67% of its matcher cycles in the generic backtracker (`lre_exec_backtrack`), which decodes every opcode on every step. The generator translates the hot patterns' bytecode to C (labels for jump targets, immediates inline, class tests inline, greedy runs as tight loops) and the engine runs that instead: regexp row -13.5% cycles at fixed work.

Soundness
  - the matcher is looked up by the exact bytecode (length and bytes), so a different compiler version or different flags give a different bytecode and the interpreter runs; with no unit registered `lre_aot_count` is 0 and nothing changes
  - the matcher runs the same machine (capture array, backtrack stack, undo log, loop counters in the capture array, run frames) from the same bytecode; the interrupt poll is kept at backward jumps, loops, runs and every backtrack; stack growth goes through the same `lre_stack_realloc`
  - patterns with lookahead, back references, 32-bit ranges or `prev` are not translated and stay interpreted; unicode-mode subjects of 16-bit units (surrogate pairs) have no 16-bit matcher
  - checked against the interpreter on 259 patterns (flags g i m s u y included), random 8-bit and 16-bit subjects up to 600 units, 199,200 runs, equal return value and capture offsets in every run

